# TEMPLATE — NOT INSTALLED BY THE PACKAGE.
#
# Reference for /etc/sudoers.d/tl-reconcile (mode 0440, owner root:root). The
# package ships this file to /usr/share/terminal-lobby/ and never onto the live
# path, for the same reason it does not ship sudoers.d-ttyd-users: the line
# names one specific account, and a copy installed elsewhere would grant root to
# whoever happens to hold that name there.
#
# Render the real one with `tl-users apply -deploy-grant`, or write it by hand.
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR
#
# CI deploys by SSH-ing to the box as the service user with a key whose
# authorized_keys entry is a forced command:
#
#   command="sudo -n /usr/local/bin/tl-reconcile",no-agent-forwarding,\
#   no-port-forwarding,no-pty,no-user-rc,restrict ssh-ed25519 AAAA... woodpecker-deploy
#
# in the SERVICE USER's authorized_keys, not root's. This grant is what lets
# that `sudo -n` succeed. Without it the key connects and the deploy fails at
# the first privileged step, which is how a box rebuilt from the package alone
# stops taking updates.
#
# HOW WIDE IT ACTUALLY IS
#
# tl-reconcile takes no arguments and never reads its own argv, so the key
# cannot steer it: SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND is ignored, and the version is not a
# parameter. What it does do is `apt-get install`, and dpkg runs maintainer
# scripts as root. So the honest description is "run whatever root code the
# configured apt source publishes", and the real bound on this grant is which
# apt source the box trusts, not the single path below.
#
# Keep it to that one path anyway. A wider grant here would be root with no
# bound at all.
#
# The placeholder is the account the units run as. Replace it.
wizard ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/tl-reconcile
